      "textEn": "295\n\nGhormaday Parashum Satunam Varmakarmukam. Like a lion among deer, he is a leader of the youth. 28 ”. Meaning of the word\n\nGhoram—Extremely frightening; Aday—Having taken in hand; Parashum—Scepter; Sa-tunam—With a flute; Varam—Shield; Karmukam—bow;\nAnvadhavat—Chased; Durmarshah—Filled with excessive anger; Mrigendrah—Lion; Iva—Like; Yuthapam—On an elephant (to attack). Taking his terrible axe, shield, bow, and quiver, the extremely angry Parashurama\nchased Kartavirya Arjuna, just as a lion chases an elephant. Tamapatantam Bhriguvaryam Ojasa\nDhanurdharam Bānaparśvadhayudham.\nWith the skin of the body, the garment of the body,\nWith the matted hair, the city is seen in full view. 29. Meaning of the word:\n\nTam—That; Apatantam—Following; Bhrigu-varyam—Bhrigu dynasty's Lord Parashurama; Ojasa—Extremely terrible; Dhanu—bow; Dharan-e-dharan; Ayan—weapon; Ayudham—all these weapons; Aineya-chamar—deer skin; Ambaram—\nCovering one's body; Ark-dhamabhih—Appearing like sunlight; Yutam Jatabhih—Adorned with matted hair; Dadrase—He saw;\nPurim—In the capital; Vishan—Entering;\nDurdur—In the city; Vishan—Entering;\nDurdur—In the city; Dadrase—He saw;\nPuri—In the capital; Vishan—Entering;\nDurdur—In the city; Vishan—Entering;\nDurdur—In the city; Vishan—Entering;\nDur Just now, King Kartavirya Arjuna was entering his capital, Mahishmati Puri, when he\nsaw Lord Parashurama, the best among the Bhrigu dynasty, chasing him with axe, shield, bow, and arrow. Parashuraji was wearing the skin of a black deer, and his matted hair appeared like the radiance of the sun.\nIt seemed. Achodayadhrathirathashvapatibhiti-\nGadasibanashtishatghnashaktibhih.\nAkshauhini, the seventeenth, fearful of ten thousand,\nRam, the one, pleased by God. 30. Meaning of words\n\nAchodayat—sent to fight; Hasti—elephant; Rath—chariot; Ashva—horses; Patibhishah— including infantry; Gada— mace; Asi— sword;\nBana— arrow; Rishti— a tool named Rishti; Shataghni— a weapon named Shataghni; 